A||N||20||XenoGen||How Commom/Rare are Fieros?||||5||
Z||000000||XenoGen||12-26-1999||12:35 PM||||Re: How Commom Are Fieros?<BR>Beware: Long post -- And its late in the night.<P>Hi all. This is my first time posting, although I've been reading about Fieros for a while. Allow me to introduce myself: I have an obssession with unique cars. Here's a little background info: I'm a college student, and have discovered an interest in cars within this past year. I didn't think I would like to drive, but when I got my license two years ago, and started driving, I found out that I really loved to drive. My favorite cars are FireBirds (I own one), Bricklins (I wish I owned one), and Fieros (I hope to own one soon.) I like to play memory games, such as "Name the Model and Year of that Fiero In Ten Seconds or Less." and "How Many Fieros Can You Spot In Five Minutes?".  I also have a habit of memorizing the locations of Fieros I spot so that I can repeatedly visit them and droole at them.)<P>My Point: I have an obvservation about my city. I think there are too many Fieros for my city's size.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I want your opinions about this. I was sortof under the assumption that Fieros were rare enough, having been made for only 5 years and being over 10 years old. But it just seems that Fieros are common cars here, and I was wondering how common they are in your cities. I live in a medium sized Canadian city (less than 60,000 people, and has 51 traffic lights). (Memory games, again.)<P><BR> To give you an example of how common Fieros are here, here is a little story:<P> Two weeks ago or so, my friends and I saw a red 84 Fiero parked on the side of a street. We turned down the street to take a closer look at it. When we got there, a few hundred yards away was anoher red Fiero. For fun, we took a closer look at that one too. From there, we saw yet ANOTHER red Fiero, and then another. Four red fieros on the same road all within a few hundred yards away from each other. This doesn't happen every day, but you usually see a bunch throught out the day.<P>A few days earlier, there was a nice looking burgendy Fiero GT parked at a Canadian Tire. I asked my friend, Tim, who was driving to drop by for a minute. He said okay, he just wanted to get gas first. A few minutes later, we strolled by the parking space - And in it's place was another Fiero of the same colour, however it was a 2m4 in poor condition, with the headlights stuck up and a black bra covering the front. (The GT that I initially saw there was in good condition and the headlights were down, and it didn't have a bra, so it wasn't just a mistaken identity.)  In the few minutes that we got gas, it seemed that one Fiero left and another took its spot.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyways, these things happen freqently enough and It just seems to me that for a car that was only made for 5 years my city sure has a LOT of them driving around...<P>On my short, daily drive to school, there are 8 Fieros that are parked on the way. Almost all my friends have at least one to four Fieros within a few houses from theirs. It seems I can't go anywheres without seeing Fieros of all kinds.<P>Is this normal, or am I just lucky?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> Maybe I just assumed Fieros were rarer than they actually are. Although I've had trouble finding them for sale... And the ones I do find for sale, the owners don't actually want to sell them until the Spring. They say they want to drive it during the Winter. Oh well.||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GT||12-26-1999||01:03 PM||||I live down near Tampa, Fl and I usually see about 1 or 2 fieros a week.  I think you just happen to be in a city that is ate up with them.  You should consider yourself lucky to see so many of them.  I wouldnt say they are rare yet, but they are definately sought after by certain individuals. 8-)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||12-26-1999||04:09 PM||||I think in some magical way someone up there is telling you TIME TO BUY A FIERO! Watch, when you do you'll probably see one like every two weeks!||||reg||6
Z||000003||overlord||12-26-1999||04:48 PM||||xenogen, where ya from?<P>just curious when u siad small town with 60000 people..||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||12-26-1999||05:55 PM||||I live in a small Canadian town, and we have about 10,000 people in the area. (just combined 3 close towns under one name, so the sign still says 6,000.)<P>I wouldn't call 60,000 people a small town.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||four50four||12-26-1999||06:10 PM||||didn't he say medium-sized town? i also live outside a medium sized canadian town (70000) and i have only every seen a fastback GT once, at that was up north, an hour away from the city. fieros are somewhat of a rarity here...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||12-26-1999||06:16 PM||||Xenogen, I spend some three weeks every year in Pittsburgh PA, and in those three weeks I see 2 or 3 Fieros, all '84s. I've also lived in San Antonio for almost a year and Fieros were pretty rare there also. Actually, in most of the cities I've visited in the States I've only seen a few.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-26-1999||08:24 PM||||Cliff--Please tell us which 3 weeks you plan on being in Pittsburgh.  I just might feel the need to take a vacation then.  I have to meet you and shake your hand.<P>XenoGen--There can NEVER be too many Fieros in one city!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I live in a major metropolis (30,000 people, 12 traffic lights) and there are only 4 Fieros here, counting the 1 I currently have, but not counting the 3 more I will have shortly.  Just a suggestion...Sell your Firebird and use the money to buy a Fiero.  It will be the best decision you will make all year!!  And next year, too, depending on when you read this post! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Vote for the Animals!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||XenoGen||12-26-1999||10:44 PM||||I live ina  highly salted city in the wintertime, and we usually get a lot of snow. Saturns are really popular here to, so I guess that could attribute to why there might be a lot of Fieros here. (NB, Canada)<P>Monkeyman: Yeah, I've been debating on selling my FireBird for a Fiero. It's my second FireBird and although I like the car, I've been wanting to go for something different. FireBirds, as you must know, are kinda big bulky wide cars - Mine's an automatic, uses a lot of gas.  So I figured I'd go to the other end of the spectrum and get a small, two seater, four cylinder, manual, Fiero.  <P> My parents of course don't like the idea of selling my car to get another car; And they do have a valid point, cause when you buy a second hand car who knows what problems might develop in a couple of months... It's a stranger to you.<P> At least with my current car I know what repairs have been done so I know what probably won't fail soon, you know? I feel that my current car would be less risky than buying a Fiero, simply because I have no idea what the car's repair history might be like or how it was driven ect.<P> But then again, risks can sometimes be a good thing. However, when one has a limited budget - That is to say just enough for the car and insurance and gas - if something were to fail, I would be completely stuck. <P> Well, getting a Fiero isn't a decision I have to make real quick, I guess. The  owners of the Fiero down the road that I really like are only selling it in the springtime, so that leaves me with time to think.  (It's an 87 Fiero SE, 4 cyl, Medium Red.)<P>   Yeah, why is the "official" colour name something like Medium Red, cause to me it really looks like a purplish marroon/burgendy. (AM I colour blind?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000009||dogtired||12-27-1999||12:01 AM||||WHoooo!  just about stepped in some monkey dung!<BR>Rarity in the Fiero community is highly debatable. Seens like you never see many when you are looking to buy, till you buy one, then they are everywhere. Then when you want to buy a parts car, they go into hiding again. and after you find one they come out again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I think they know and try to FOOL us humans.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  So don't act human and maybe you will find more Fiero's like MonkeyMan & me. Animals R US!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000010||Silver Bullet||12-28-1999||06:51 PM||||I agree with dogtired.  I didn't see very many at all until I bought mine.  Now they are everywhere.  The cool thing about it though is that everybody loves their Fieros and you can actually hunt down a fiero owner and make friends with them.  It sounds corny, but it is actually pretty cool.  My only advice, is to by a V6.  They are more fun.  (No offense to anybody)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cooter||12-28-1999||07:51 PM||||A fiero is rare when you narrow what you want to a specific model or options for a particular year. I had been looking for an 88 Formula or GT and found everything except what I was looking for. If you are not picky about year or options, they are fairly common. If you want a yellow 88 formula with t-tops, leather and manual windows, you might have to keep looking.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fiero SE||12-28-1999||09:12 PM||||I just got back from Port ST.Lucie Florida today (12-28-99) and I only seen3 Fiero's the whole time. one was under a tarp and 2 were on the road. I seen one of them in West Palm Beach when we were on the way to the Lotus dealer for Picutres. I wanted to take pictures of me by some exotics but a lotus espirit V8 was as close as I got, though I did see a black Ferrari 355 F1 at a gas station. But in Oklahoma Fiero are fairly popular. I've seen quite a few here. But I'd have to say that they are more popular in some places than others but probably are becoming rare because of collectors and people wrecking them.||||reg||10
Z||000013||Monkeyman||12-28-1999||10:34 PM||||If you're asking if they're rare like a Ferrari Daytona, then no.  But are they as common as an Escort?  No, again.  I think you could consider them slightly rare (how's that for straddling the fence?!).  They were only built for 5 years, only about 325,000 were made, and they are out of production.  Maybe a better question would be, "How sought after are they?".  Any unique car that is out of production would be sought after.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Voyd||12-29-1999||03:01 AM||||id say they are kinda rare. rare enough to only see one once a week or thereabouts. depends on where you live tho i guess. i know where i live (east coast canada) theres alot of snow, rain, mud, all that crap during the winter and the fiero's non rusting body makes it appealing. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||12-29-1999||03:15 AM||||The bodies don't rust, but the frames do.  Keep that in mind next time you wash your baby and give it a good hosing underneath.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Shiner||12-29-1999||03:49 AM||||Monkey,<BR> I wanted to powder coat the entire frame red, but I can't find a big enough oven!  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||ChadMan||12-29-1999||09:52 AM||||Columbus seems to be teeming with Fieros - though the nicest ones only come out when the weather's nice.<P>There are three Fieros in my parking lot at work - an 87 GT, an 84, and mine.  I'm proud, because I'm the only one with working headlights!<P>I used to work for a major adult-nutritional firm here in town, and there were five Fieros there.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||12-29-1999||10:56 AM||||Shiner--I'm all for improving a Fiero and powder coating the frame would be trick, but do you know how much work it would be to strip the frame completely?  Whew!!  What a job!  I wish you all the luck.  If you decide to do it, make sure you take plenty of pics to show us (before, during and after).  BTW-There are plenty of powder coaters with big enough ovens.  Ask a local body shop for some suggestions.  It might take a lot of hunting, but you'll find one eventually.||||reg||1
